Object.assign(MAP_EN.desc,{"西班牙午睡": "Spain has a tradition of an afternoon nap. Most city office workers no longer take one, but many small shops still close in the afternoon.", "地中海饮食": "Lots of vegetables, beans, olive oil and fish. UNESCO listed it as intangible cultural heritage in 2010.", "英国的「孤独部长」": "In 2018 the UK appointed the world's first government minister for loneliness.", "极夜里的光疗灯": "For almost two months in winter the sun never rises, so many people use light-therapy lamps to fight the winter blues.", "丹麦 hygge": "Candles, friends and taking it slow: the cosy feeling Danes use to get through winter.", "地上的红绿灯": "So many people walk while looking at their phones that Seoul put traffic lights into the pavement.", "冰岛少上班试验": "From 2015 to 2019 Iceland trialled a 35–36 hour week; in most workplaces productivity did not drop.", "森林浴": "The word \"forest bathing\" (shinrin-yoku) was coined in Japan in 1982; the Akasawa forest in Nagano is called its birthplace.", "优衣库银座店": "Uniqlo's Ginza flagship has 12 floors; its basics are sold from here to the whole world.", "牛津街": "Often called Europe's busiest shopping street, with queues for the fitting rooms all along it.", "耐克总部": "Nike's headquarters is in Oregon, not New York or Los Angeles.", "下北泽古着街": "Dozens of vintage clothing shops on a few streets, where young people hunt for second-hand clothes.", "土耳其理发店": "After your haircut, the barber singes the fine hair off your ears with a flame.", "双十一": "A shopping festival started by Alibaba in 2009 on 11 November, now one of the biggest online shopping days in the world.", "明洞化妆品街": "A street full of Korean cosmetics shops, with staff handing out free samples at the door.", "日本的「清凉商务」": "Since 2005 Japan has encouraged office workers to skip the tie in summer and set the air-con to 28°C.", "旧金山的 AI 公司": "Many of the companies building AI assistants have their headquarters in San Francisco.", "萝卜快跑": "In Wuhan you can hail a taxi with no driver in it.", "苹果总部 Apple Park": "Apple's headquarters is one giant ring-shaped building.", "韩国电竞": "Korea's League of Legends league has its own arena, and matches feel as lively as a football game.", "CES 消费电子展": "Every January the world's new headphones, laptops and gadgets are shown off here.", "欧盟 GDPR": "A privacy law from 2018 — it is why websites keep asking whether you accept cookies.", "日本药妆店": "The drugstores around Shinsaibashi are among tourists' favourite places to shop.", "匈牙利看牙": "Many Europeans travel to Hungary just to see a dentist, because it costs much less.", "美国急诊账单": "Without insurance, one trip to the emergency room can cost over a thousand dollars.", "曼谷的私立医院": "Many foreigners fly to Bangkok for treatment: it is fast and cheaper than at home.", "印度血清研究所": "The company that makes the most vaccine doses in the world is in Pune, India.", "日本的公司体检": "Japanese law requires companies to give every employee a health check once a year.", "古巴医生": "Cuba has one of the highest numbers of doctors per person in the world.", "扫码付钱": "In China people pay for groceries and buses by scanning a QR code; Alipay's headquarters is in Hangzhou.", "德国「各付各的」": "After a meal in Germany the waiter often asks: \"Together or separately?\"", "日本新 NISA": "A new tax-free investment account started in 2024, and many young people bought their first funds.", "美国的信用分": "In the US, renting a flat, buying a car or getting a phone plan all depend on your credit score.", "德国人爱用现金": "Germans are famous for loving cash, and many small shops take cash only.", "外卖骑手": "China has millions of food delivery riders, and an algorithm decides how fast they must deliver.", "全世界最低的生育率": "In 2023 South Korean women had on average just 0.72 children, the lowest in the world.", "柏林的房租冻结": "Berlin froze rents in 2020; the next year the constitutional court struck the law down.", "西班牙圣诞彩票": "The draw is on 22 December every year, and the whole country watches TV waiting for the big prize.", "丹麦的高税": "Denmark has some of the highest income taxes in the world, and university and healthcare are free.", "加拿大全民医保": "You don't pay at the doctor's, but waiting for a specialist can take a long time.", "法国延迟退休": "In 2023 France raised the retirement age from 62 to 64, setting off nationwide strikes.", "澳大利亚最低工资": "Australia has one of the highest minimum wages in the world.", "原宿竹下通": "A narrow street of crêpe stands, rainbow candy floss and other viral shops, packed at weekends.", "坎登市场": "London's famous market, with vintage clothes, street food and handmade stalls side by side.", "李子坝轻轨穿楼": "A light-rail line runs straight through a block of flats, and tourists queue across the road to take photos.", "春运": "Before Chinese New Year there are billions of trips as people go home to their parents.", "慢城运动": "A \"slow town\" movement that began in a small Italian town in 1999: eat slowly, live slowly.", "真人图书馆": "Started in Copenhagen in 2000: you borrow a person instead of a book, and listen to their story.", "韩国高考": "During the English listening test, planes across the country stop taking off and landing.", "日本的入社式": "Most new graduates in Japan start work on the same day, 1 April, with a welcome ceremony.", "瑞典一个人住": "About four in ten Swedish households are one person living alone.", "parkrun 公园跑": "Started in London's Bushy Park in 2004: 5 km every Saturday morning, free, open to everyone.", "书镇海伊": "A small town with over twenty second-hand bookshops and a book festival every year.", "上胜町垃圾分类": "This small town sorts its rubbish into more than 40 categories.", "香港的劏房": "One flat is split into several tiny rooms for rent, some only a few square metres.", "德国超市周日关门": "Most German supermarkets are closed on Sundays, so you shop on Saturday.", "迪拜的外国人": "About nine in ten people in Dubai are foreigners.", "世界煮粥大赛": "Since 1994 the Highland village of Carrbridge has hosted the World Porridge Making Championship every October. The prize is a golden stirring stick, the Golden Spurtle. The traditional recipe is just oats, water and salt.", "家里扔掉的饭": "The UN Environment Programme (based in Nairobi) says in its Food Waste Index Report 2024 that about 1.05 billion tonnes of food were wasted in 2022. Around 60% came from homes, about 79 kg per person a year, much of it leftovers and food that went off.", "伦敦博罗市场": "A food market beside London Bridge; the market says food has been sold on this spot for about a thousand years. Today it sells seasonal fruit and veg, cheese and bread to locals, and street food to tourists.", "美国的小费越来越多": "A 2023 Pew Research Center survey found 72% of Americans say tips are now expected in more places than five years ago, even on screens when you buy a coffee. Many say they are unsure how much to give.", "中国的猫比狗多了": "China's 2023 Pet Industry White Paper counts about 69.8 million pet cats and 51.8 million pet dogs in cities. Cats overtook dogs in 2021; young people in small flats with busy jobs tend to choose cats.", "葡萄为什么会毒倒狗": "Grapes and raisins can cause kidney failure in dogs. In 2021 the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center said the likely culprit is tartaric acid in the fruit. So keep the grapes off the floor.", "英国的猫也要打芯片": "Since 10 June 2024, cats in England must be microchipped by 20 weeks old, or owners can be fined up to £500 (dogs have needed chips since 2016). If a cat goes missing while you are away, the chip is how it gets home.", "猫为什么爱钻盒子": "A 2021 study from Hunter College, New York (Applied Animal Behaviour Science) asked owners to put an illusion of a square on the floor. Many cats went and sat inside it anyway: cats seem drawn to anything that looks like a box.", "中央公园的不拴绳时间": "New York City Parks rules say dogs in Central Park must be leashed, except before 9 am and after 9 pm in set areas. That is why the park is full of dog walkers at dawn.", "英国查兽医收费": "Vet bills keep rising, so the UK Competition and Markets Authority began an investigation in 2024. Many owners don't know their practice belongs to a big chain, or that medicine can be cheaper elsewhere. In 2025 it proposed that practices publish price lists.", "英国宠物店不卖小狗了": "Since April 2020, Lucy's Law in England bans pet shops and dealers from selling puppies and kittens under six months. You must buy straight from the breeder or adopt from a rescue. It is named after Lucy, a dog rescued from a puppy farm.", "台北的猫咖": "Cat Flower Garden, opened in Taipei in 1998, is often called the world's first cat café. Japanese visitors took the idea to Tokyo, and now London and New York have them too; many work with shelters so you can adopt a cat you like.", "缅甸的电诈园区": "A 2023 UN human rights report said at least 120,000 people were trapped in scam compounds in Myanmar, many lured by fake well-paid jobs and forced to defraud people online. In 2025 Thailand cut power to border compounds, and Myanmar's army said it raided KK Park near Myawaddy.", "买手机的国家补贴": "From 20 January 2025, China subsidises new phones, tablets and smartwatches: items under 6,000 yuan get 15% off, up to 500 yuan each. Many people used it to upgrade their phones.", "牛津年度词「brain rot」": "Oxford University Press chose 'brain rot' as its 2024 Word of the Year: the feeling your mind is rotting from scrolling low-quality videos and memes. It said use of the word rose 230% in a year.", "skibidi 进了剑桥词典": "In August 2025 the Cambridge Dictionary added internet words such as skibidi, delulu and tradwife. Its editors said they add words that people really use and that look likely to stay.", "充电宝上飞机的新规": "From 28 June 2025, China's aviation regulator banned power banks without the 3C safety mark, or recalled ones, from domestic flights. Emirates banned using power banks on board from October 2025. Check yours before you pack.", "迪拜机场": "According to Airports Council International, Dubai International is the world's busiest airport for international passengers, with about 92.3 million in 2024. Many people change planes here between Europe, Asia and Africa.", "胶囊旅馆": "The world's first capsule hotel opened in Umeda, Osaka, in 1979, designed by architect Kisho Kurokawa for office workers who missed the last train. Now many tourists stay a night just to try it.", "谷歌地图在韩国不好用": "South Korea, citing security, won't let Google take detailed map data abroad, so Google Maps can't give walking or driving directions there. Locals will tell you to use Naver Map or KakaoMap.", "年轻人找工作难": "China's National Bureau of Statistics said unemployment among 16-to-24-year-olds not in school hit 18.9% in August 2025, the highest since this measure began. Many graduates say they send hundreds of CVs to get one interview.", "日本告别负利率": "In March 2024 the Bank of Japan raised rates for the first time in 17 years, ending negative interest rates, and in January 2025 it raised them to 0.5%. Savings finally earn a little, but mortgages may cost more.", "OPEC 开会定产量": "OPEC is based in Vienna. With Russia and other producers (together OPEC+) it decides how much oil to pump each day. Less oil means higher prices, all the way to the petrol station. In 2025 they began raising output step by step.", "洛杉矶港": "The Port of Los Angeles is the busiest container port in the US, landing huge amounts of goods from Asia. When the US raised tariffs sharply in 2025, importers rushed shipments in before the deadlines, then container numbers suddenly dropped.", "全球教师奖": "Since 2015 the Varkey Foundation has given a US$1 million Global Teacher Prize each year, often called the Nobel for teachers. Winners are often ordinary teachers working in remote places, refugee camps or poor neighbourhoods.", "英国的食物银行": "Trussell, Britain's largest food bank network (based in Salisbury), says it gave out about 2.9 million emergency food parcels from April 2024 to March 2025. Most of the sorting and handing out is done by local volunteers.", "班克斯画在法院墙上": "In September 2025 the street artist Banksy painted a judge beating a protester with a gavel on a wall of the Royal Courts of Justice in London. It was covered up and removed within days. Nobody knows who Banksy is, but every new work makes the news.", "东京都政府办的约会 App": "With births in Japan falling year after year, the Tokyo Metropolitan Government launched its own dating app in 2024. Users must show proof that they are single and of their income, and do a video interview. Some find it trustworthy; others say it's not the government's job.", "新加坡给 40 岁以上的人学新本事": "Since 2024 Singapore has given citizens aged 40 and over S$4,000 in credit (SkillsFuture Level-Up) to go back to study for a new career, with support while they retrain.", "抗生素治不了感冒": "Colds and flu are caused by viruses; antibiotics only work on bacteria. A 2025 World Health Organization report found that one in six bacterial infections worldwide is resistant to common antibiotics, and misuse is one reason why.", "多邻国的连续打卡": "Pittsburgh-based Duolingo made the daily 'streak' its signature, with the owl nagging you not to break it. In 2025 its CEO said the company would go 'AI-first' and use fewer contractors, and many users protested online.", "考研的人变少了": "China's Ministry of Education says sign-ups for the postgraduate entrance exam peaked at 4.74 million in 2023 and fell to 3.88 million in 2025. Some choose civil-service exams or jobs; others feel degrees are worth less and learn a practical skill instead.", "首尔的「陪你学习」直播": "'Study with me' videos are huge in South Korea: creators film themselves quietly studying for hours with a timer on screen. Many viewers say having one on is what stops them putting things off.", "「放弃日」": "The fitness app Strava, based in San Francisco, looked at hundreds of millions of workouts and found many New Year's resolutions don't survive past the second Friday of January. It named that day Quitter's Day.", "柏林的语言交换 App": "Tandem, a language-exchange app based in Berlin, pairs you with someone who teaches you their language while you teach them yours. Berlin is full of foreigners, and bars hold language-exchange nights: half the time German, half English.", "《鱿鱼游戏》": "The Korean drama Squid Game's first season is Netflix's most-watched series ever, and its third and final season came out in June 2025. For many people around the world it was the first foreign-language show they watched with subtitles.", "格拉斯顿伯里的休息年": "Glastonbury Festival is held on a working farm and takes a year off every few years to let the land and local villages rest. 2026 is one of those fallow years; the next festival is in 2027.", "东京公务员可以上四天班": "Since April 2025, Tokyo Metropolitan Government staff can choose to work four days a week and get a three-day weekend. The government says it wants to help people balance work and childcare, and to encourage people to have children.", "2026 年世界杯": "The summer 2026 World Cup was the first with 48 teams and 104 matches, co-hosted by the US, Canada and Mexico, with the final at MetLife Stadium near New York. Many fans around the world got up in the middle of the night to watch.", "一个人去唱 K": "In Japan going to karaoke alone is normal enough to have a name, 'hitokara'. Tokyo even has chains just for solo singers, with tiny booths and headphones, so no one hears you go off-key."});
